The '''Constitutional Amendment on Sex-Based Discrimination''' ({{lang|ca|Esmena Constitucional sobre la Discriminació per Sexe}}), colloquially known as the '''Thirteenth Guarantee''', is a {{w|constitutional amendment}} to the [[Twelve Guarantees]] of [[Balboa]] passed by the [[National Assembly of Balboa|National Assembly]] in 1986. The amendment added sex to the list of protected categories under the Guarantees' anti-discrimination framework, prohibiting discrimination on the basis of sex in employment, housing, education, healthcare, and access to goods and services. It was the first expansion of the Guarantees' scope since their original adoption in 1960 and established the precedent that the Guarantees could be amended to address emerging forms of discrimination.

== Background ==

The [[Twelve Guarantees]], negotiated in 1960 as a condition for the return of the [[International Mandate of Sant Cristòfor|Sant Cristòfor mandate]] to Balboan sovereignty, established constitutional protections against discrimination on the basis of race, ethnicity, religion, and national origin. The Guarantees did not include sex as a protected category. The omission was noted at the time of the negotiations, but the deliberations were dominated by the racial and ethnic dimensions of the Balboan conflict, and the inclusion of sex-based protections was not pursued.

Following reunification in 1973, the 1974 constitution incorporated the Twelve Guarantees but did not expand their scope. Women's organisations argued that the Guarantees' general anti-discrimination language could be interpreted to cover sex-based discrimination, but courts consistently held that the enumerated categories were exhaustive. The 1978 ruling in ''[[Margall v. Sant Cristòfor Municipal Credit Union]]'' confirmed this position, when a married woman was denied a business loan because the credit union required her husband's co-signature, and the court held that the Guarantees did not cover sex-based discrimination.

== Campaign ==

The campaign for a constitutional amendment was led by a coalition of urban and rural women's organisations. The urban women's movement, centred in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and [[Sant Bart]], included professional women's associations, {{w|trade union}} caucuses, and {{w|legal aid}} societies. The rural women's movement, which proved decisive in building political pressure, was rooted in the {{w|cooperative|cooperative farming}} sector of [[Jala Province|Jala]], [[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]], and [[Nurra Province|Nurra]], where women were systematically excluded from cooperative governance despite contributing substantially to agricultural labour.

The 1983 ruling in ''[[Salvà v. Grup Llibertat]]'', in which a court upheld a cooperative's exclusion of women from board elections, demonstrated that the absence of constitutional protections enabled discrimination in both public and private institutions. The ruling catalysed the rural women's movement and led to the organisation of the 1984 National Women's March, which drew an estimated 5,000 women to [[Sant Cristòfor]] and is widely regarded as the event that forced the National Assembly to begin serious consideration of the amendment.

President [[Roser Muntaner i Ferrer]], elected in 1983 as the first woman President of Balboa, championed the amendment and used her legislative address to urge the National Assembly to pass it, framing it as a necessary completion of the Guarantees' promise of equality.

== Provisions ==

The amendment added sex to the protected categories under the [[Twelve Guarantees]] framework. It prohibited {{w|discrimination}} on the basis of sex in employment, housing, education, healthcare, and access to goods and services. Complaints were to be adjudicated by the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]], which had the power to order remedies including compensation, reinstatement, and {{w|injunction}}s.

The amendment did not address {{w|reproductive rights}}, {{w|domestic violence|violence against women}}, or {{w|political representation|women's political representation}}. Women's organisations had pushed for the inclusion of reproductive rights in the amendment, but the provision was dropped to secure the {{w|supermajority}} required for passage. These issues were left to subsequent legislation, including the Reproductive Health Care Act (1989), the Domestic Violence Prevention Act (1993), and the Electoral Parity Act (1998).

== Text ==

The amendment reads as follows:

{{Quote|'''Thirteenth Guarantee''' — No person shall be subjected to discrimination on the basis of sex in any sphere of public life, including employment, housing, education, healthcare, and the provision of goods and services. The National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal shall have jurisdiction to adjudicate complaints arising under this Guarantee and to order such remedies as are necessary to remedy the effects of discrimination, including compensation, reinstatement, and injunctions.}}

== Passage ==

The amendment was introduced in the National Assembly by [[Caterina Vives i Rosell]], a [[Balboan People's Party|BPP]] member representing the Jala–Sant Bart constituency. The amendment passed with the required {{w|supermajority}} and was ratified by [[Adonian Community]] review. No opposition was recorded in the final vote, though conservative and religious groups had expressed reservations during the debate.

== Legacy ==

The amendment was the first expansion of the Twelve Guarantees since their original adoption in 1960. It established the precedent that the Guarantees could be amended to address emerging forms of discrimination, a precedent later followed by the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sexual Orientation Protections]] (2008) and the [[Constitutional Amendment on Gender Identity Protections]] (2019).

The first successful application of the amendment to an employment discrimination case was ''[[Reig v. Ministry of Education]]'' (1990), in which the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] ruled that a teacher who had been passed over for promotion in favour of a less-qualified male colleague was entitled to promotion and back pay. The ruling established that the burden of proof in sex-based discrimination cases rested with the employer.

